        "rationale": "The text has one structural obligation: \"The `secretshape` version MUST be pinned exactly because changes to its rules can affect evidence hashes.\" A dependency declared as `secretshape = \"0.1\"` or `\"^0.1.0\"` could produce the same runtime results today but would let the version drift. That would violate the clause, so the clause is structural. The supplied root, `crates/trust-but-verify/Cargo.toml#table[dependencies]/pair[secretshape]`, declares `secretshape = \"=0.1.0\"`. This is an exact-equality requirement and is marked `{impl}`. It satisfies the clause. This is the only place where the dependency is declared as far as I could see. The `Cargo.lock` entries only record the resolved version. I found no other manifest declaring the crate.\n\nThe remaining clauses specify observable behavior only:\n- Applying the credential rules to mandatory inputs, tool reads, search results, the book, `habeas info` and configuration files.\n- Replacing each match with `[REDACTED:<rule name>]` while preserving line breaks.\n- Extending a match through whitespace-adjacent entropy regions.\n- Never replacing on entropy alone.\n- Outlines using the original source for structure and spans, and structural hashes using the redacted text.\n\nAny implementation that produces the same outputs satisfies them. One clause names the `secretshape` rules. Using the crate is implied by the exact-pin clause, and the dependency root covers that. The redaction code in `crates/trust-but-verify/src/redaction/mod.rs` (`use secretshape::Scanner`, `fn[scanners]`, `fn[secret_regions]`) delivers the behavior. I treated it as behavioral and did not require a marker there. I did not assess test adequacy. Tests are outside this assessment."
